.TH SNIF 1 "October 2026" "snif 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
snif \- Sniff Network Interface Frames, a packet decoder with one color by layer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snif
[\fB\-hvpedtsSnlargKx81\fR]
[\fB\-T rtp\fR]
[\fB\-\-tcpdump\fR]
[\fB\-i\fR \fIinterface\fR]
[\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR]
[\fB\-c\fR \fIcount\fR]
[\fB\-w\fR \fIfile\fR [\fB\-C\fR \fIsize\fR] [\fB\-G\fR \fIseconds\fR] [\fB\-W\fR \fIcount\fR]]
[\fB\-Q\fR \fIin\fR|\fIout\fR|\fIinout\fR]
[\fB\-B\fR \fIsize\fR]
[\fB\-D#I\fR]
[\fIfilter expression\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snif
captures the packets of a network interface, or reads them from a pcap
file written by
.BR tcpdump (1)
or Wireshark, and shows them layer after layer, from the link layer to
the application, each layer in its own color.
.PP
It only decodes and displays: it does not reassemble the TCP streams
(a message cut over several segments is decoded only in its first
segment) and does no traffic analysis.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Show the help and exit.
.TP
.BI \-i " interface"
Capture on this interface, for instance
.BR eth0 ,
.B wlp0s20f3
or
.B any
(all the interfaces, as a Linux cooked capture).
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read the packets from a pcap file instead of an interface.
.TP
.BI \-c " count"
Exit after
.I count
packets.
.TP
.BI \-w " file"
Write the packets to a pcap file, as
.BR "tcpdump \-w" ,
to read them later with
.B \-f
or Wireshark; they are not shown, unless
.B \-\-print
is given too. With
.B \-f
and a filter, it extracts packets from a file.
.TP
.BI \-C " size"
With
.BR \-w ,
start a new file above
.I size
million bytes: file, file1, file2...
.TP
.BI \-G " seconds"
With
.BR \-w ,
start a new file every
.I seconds
(time of the packets); the name may hold the time, as in
.BR strftime (3):
.IR dump\-%H%M%S.pcap .
.TP
.BI \-W " count"
With
.BR \-C ,
keep a ring of
.I count
files, the oldest being overwritten; with
.B \-G
alone, stop after
.I count
files.
.TP
.B \-\-print
With
.BR \-w ,
show the packets too.
.TP
.B \-D
List the interfaces snif can capture on, and exit.
.TP
.BI \-Q " in" | out | inout
Capture only the packets received, sent, or both.
.TP
.BI \-B " size"
Size of the kernel buffer, in KiB, where the packets wait to be read.
.TP
.B \-I
Wi-Fi monitor mode: the card captures all the 802.11 frames of its channel
(beacons, probes, associations, WPA handshakes, frames of the other
stations), with their radiotap header. It may cut the Wi-Fi connection
during the capture, and the card must support it.
.TP
.BR \-# ", " \-\-number
Show the number of each packet.
.TP
.B \-\-absolute
Show the absolute TCP sequence and acknowledgement numbers. By default,
as in
.BR tcpdump ,
they are relative to the start of the connection (1, 518...): the segments
without ACK and the first segment seen keep their absolute numbers;
in verbose mode the absolute numbers are added in parentheses.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose mode: all the fields of the headers, the options, the flags.
.B \-vv
and
.B \-vvv
are accepted and show the same.
.TP
.B \-A
Show each packet in ASCII after its decoding, without its link layer
header, as
.BR "tcpdump \-A" .
.TP
.BI \-m " text"
Show only the packets whose data hold this text. The headers (link, IP,
TCP, UDP) are not searched, only what follows them: for the other IP
protocols (ICMP, tunnels...), what follows the IP header. ARP never matches. Each packet is
searched alone: a text cut between two packets is not found.
.TP
.BI \-M " hex"
Same with bytes in hexadecimal:
.B \-M "de ad be ef"
or
.BR "\-M 0xdeadbeef" .
.TP
.BI \-F " file"
Read the capture filter from a file; from a # to the end of a line is a
comment.
.TP
.BI \-Z " user"
When started as root, run as this user once the capture is open.
.TP
.B \-p
Do not put the interface into promiscuous mode.
.TP
.B \-e
Do not show the vendor of the Ethernet cards.
.TP
.B \-d
Do not show the link layer (Ethernet addresses...).
.TP
.B \-t
Do not show the time stamps. By default, the date and time of each packet
are shown to the microsecond (the time only with
.BR \-1 ).
As in
.BR tcpdump ,
.B \-tt
shows the seconds since 1970,
.B \-ttt
the time since the previous packet,
.B \-tttt
the date and time as 2026\-11\-01 10:57:03.123456, and
.B \-ttttt
the time since the first packet.
.TP
.B \-1
One line by packet: the main line of each layer, in its color, one after
the other (with
.BR \-S ,
each one after its symbol); the details are left out. The TCP flags and
the DNS questions and answers are kept on the line.
.TP
.B \-\-tcpdump
One line by packet in the format of
.BR tcpdump ,
in colors: the address in the color of IP and the port in the color of
TCP or UDP
.RB ( "IP acer.51000 > 17.253.144.10.https: Flags [S.], ..." ),
the TCP options, DNS and ARP as tcpdump shows them. The link layer is
left out. The addresses get the names known without sending anything,
unlike
.BR tcpdump ,
whose reverse DNS queries appear in its own capture: the name of this
machine for its addresses,
.IR /etc/hosts ,
and the names learned from the DNS, mDNS and LLMNR answers seen in the
capture.
.TP
.B \-s
No escape sequences: no colors.
.TP
.B \-S
Show a symbol before the first line of each layer, with or without the
colors: in UTF-8 when the locale is, in ASCII otherwise.
.TP
.B \-8
Use only the 8 basic colors, even if the terminal knows the palette of
256 colors.
.TP
.B \-n
Print an empty line between the packets (useful without colors).
.TP
.B \-l
Show the TCP and UDP ports as numbers only, without the service name.
.TP
.B \-g
Also name the ports greater than or equal to 1024.
.TP
.B \-a
Do not load the tables of ports and vendors (saves memory).
.TP
.B \-r
Resolve the IP addresses into host names.
.TP
.B \-K
Do not verify the TCP and UDP checksums. Without
.BR \-K ,
the checksums of the packets sent by the machine itself are not verified
anyway: the network card computes them after the capture (checksum
offloading).
.TP
.B \-x
Dump each packet in hexadecimal and ASCII after its decoding.
.TP
.B \-T rtp
Decode all the UDP datagrams of version 2 as RTP and RTCP, as
.B tcpdump \-T rtp
does. Without it, RTP is recognized by its header: the header extensions
of WebRTC, or the even ports and the usual codecs of the SIP phones;
RTCP by its packet types and lengths.
.SH FILTER EXPRESSION
The arguments following the options make a capture filter, in the syntax
of
.BR tcpdump (1),
described in
.BR pcap-filter (7):
only the packets matching it are shown.
.SH COLORS
When the terminal says it knows the palette of 256 colors
.RB ( TERM
ending in
.BR \-256color ,
or
.B COLORTERM
set, as in xfce4\-terminal, gnome\-terminal or konsole), each layer has
two shades: one for its first line, a darker one for its details.
Otherwise, and with
.BR \-8 ,
the 8 basic colors are used.
.PP
.TS
l l l l.
Layer	256 colors	8 colors	\-S (ASCII)
link (Ethernet, Wi\-Fi...)	soft green	green	#
network (IP, IPv6, ARP)	golden yellow	yellow	@
ICMP, ICMPv6, IGMP	light red	red	!
TCP, SCTP	turquoise	cyan	>
UDP	cornflower blue	blue	>
applications	pale magenta	magenta	*
tunnels (GRE, VXLAN...)	orange	magenta	\&=
routing (OSPF, BGP...)	light purple	magenta	+
.TE
.PP
In UTF-8, the symbols are \[u25AA] \[u25C6] \[u2716] \[u25B8] \[u273B] \[u25CE] \[u2726] in the same order
(TCP and UDP share the same one).
The colors are defined in
.B color.h
in the sources.
.SH LIVE CAPTURE
Capturing on an interface needs the capture privileges: run
.B snif
with
.BR sudo .
On Linux, they can also be given to the binary once (again after each build):
.PP
.RS
sudo setcap cap_net_raw,cap_net_admin=eip /usr/local/sbin/snif
.RE
.PP
macOS has no
.BR setcap :
capturing needs read access to
.IR /dev/bpf* ,
which only root has by default.
.PP
Reading a file needs no privilege.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/snif/portnumbers.dat
Names of the TCP and UDP ports.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/snif/ieee_ethercodes.dat
Vendors of the Ethernet cards (IEEE registries).
.PP
When they are not installed,
.B snif
reads them in
.I ./var
(run from the source directory).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B snif \-i eth0 port 53
Show the DNS packets of eth0.
.TP
.B snif \-v \-f capture.pcap
Decode a capture file in verbose mode.
.TP
.B snif \-s \-S \-f capture.pcap > decoded.txt
Write the decoding without colors, with the symbols of the layers.
.TP
.B snif \-i any \-x host 192.168.1.1
Show the packets to or from 192.168.1.1 on all the interfaces, with
their hexadecimal dump.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tcpdump (1),
.BR pcap-filter (7),
.BR setcap (8)
